    "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\n`FLASHINFER_MLA_SPARSE_SM90` passes pageable CPU tensors (qo/kv indptr, KV lengths) to `BatchMLAPagedAttentionWrapper.plan()`. The wrapper copies them to the GPU with `non_blocking=True`, so `VLLM_GPU_SYNC_CHECK=error` rejects the copy as an implicit sync. On GLM-5.3-Flash on H200 the engine dies on its first request. This PR stages the tensors in pinned memory, the same pattern #54660 applied to the FlashInfer backend.\n\n## Claims\n\n- GLM-5.3-Flash (H200, SM90 sparse MLA) now runs under `VLLM_GPU_SYNC_CHECK=error`. Before this change it failed on the first request.\n- Every host tensor `plan()` copies to the device is now pinned. The reused staging buffers stay unpinned, as in `flashinfer.py`.\n- No performance claim: DEP8 decode throughput is neutral within run-to-run noise (table below).\n\n## Validation\n\n**Reproducer** (GLM-5.3-Flash, TP4 + MTP3, 4\u00d7H200):\n\n    VLLM_GPU_SYNC_CHECK=error vllm serve zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash \\\n      --tensor-parallel-size 4 --max-num-seqs 64 --no-enable-prefix-caching \\\n      --limit-mm-per-prompt '{\"image\":0,\"video\":0}' \\\n      --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"mtp\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":3}'\n    vllm bench serve --backend vllm --model zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash \\\n      --dataset-name speed_bench --speed-bench-dataset-subset throughput_1k \\\n      --speed-bench-output-len 256 --num-prompts 64 --max-concurrency 16\n\n| | implicit-sync errors | requests completed |\n|---|---|---|\n| main | 17. The engine died: `Tensor.copy_: a non_blocking H2D copy that may be silently synchronous (the CPU tensor is not pinned)`, raised from FlashInfer `_stage_metadata` under `_SM90State._plan` | 0 / 64 |\n| this PR | 0 | 64 / 64 |\n\n**Unit tests** (H200): `pytest tests/v1/attention/test_flashinfer_mla_sparse_sm90.py`: 25 passed. `test_plan_uses_state_params` now asserts that the tensors handed to the wrapper are pinned whenever pinned memory is available.\n\n**Performance** (DEP8 decode-only, 8\u00d7H200, DecodeBenchConnector, ISL 128K / OSL 512, MTP3 with synthetic acceptance [0.8, 0.6, 0.5], warm pass). C=128 was measured twice; the other points once.\n\n| C | main tok/s, TPOT ms | this PR tok/s, TPOT ms |\n|---|---|---|\n| 64 | 3534, 12.89 | 3927, 11.45 |\n| 128 | 4867, 17.48 | 5137, 17.04 |\n| 192 | 5684, 20.53 | 5641, 20.29 |\n\n## Details\n\n`_SM90State._plan` fills reusable CPU buffers (`_qo_cpu`, `_kv_cpu`, `_lens_cpu`) in place and passes them to the wrapper. With `use_cuda_graph=True`, the wrapper's `_stage_metadata` copies them into its reserved device buffers with `non_blocking=True`. From pageable memory CUDA may synchronize to stage that copy, which is what the sync check flags.\n\nThe fix passes a `.pin_memory()` copy of each buffer instead of making the buffers themselves pinned. PyTorch's caching host allocator keeps a pinned block alive until the async copy that reads it completes, so the next plan cannot overwrite in-flight data. Pinning the reused buffers directly would need explicit events to avoid that race. This mirrors `vllm/v1/attention/backends/flashinfer.py` since #54660.\n\nNot a duplicate: no open PR touches this backend's plan staging. #58684 removed this plan's D2H sync; this change covers the H2D side.\n\nOut of scope: `plan()` itself costs 4\u20136.5 ms per call, measured without the profiler. Most of that is FlashInfer's Python workload validation (`_fa_plan_work_count`), which runs over all `max_num_batched_tokens` rows, twice per plan. That belongs in a FlashInfer change.\n\nAI assistance (Claude Code) was used to prepare this change. I reviewed every changed line and ran the validation above.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
